  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The brain is covered by several membranes. A <strong>meningioma</strong> grows from cells of the arachnoid membrane and is usually attached to the dura mater. Most often it does not invade the brain but pushes it aside and compresses it, growing slowly — over years.</p>
    <p>It is one of the most common tumours of the central nervous system — up to a quarter of all cases. Meningioma is found about twice as often in women, usually aged 40–60.</p>
    <h3>Why it occurs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Radiation to the head</strong>, especially in childhood, is a proven risk factor.</li>
      <li><strong>Hormones</strong> are linked to the higher rate in women, including around menopause.</li>
      <li><strong>Neurofibromatosis type 2</strong> — a rare genetic condition in which there are several meningiomas.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In other cases the tumour arises from random changes in cells. Having a relative with meningioma does not, as a rule, raise the risk.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>A small meningioma often causes no symptoms and is found by chance — on an MRI done for another reason. As the tumour grows, symptoms depend on where it is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>headache</strong>, more often in the morning, sometimes with nausea and vomiting;</li>
      <li><strong>seizures</strong> — may be the first sign;</li>
      <li><strong>vision problems</strong>: double vision, loss of parts of the visual field — if the tumour is near the optic nerves;</li>
      <li><strong>weakness or numbness</strong> of an arm or leg;</li>
      <li><strong>changes in memory, attention, behaviour</strong> — with frontal and temporal tumours;</li>
      <li><strong>loss of smell</strong> — with tumours at the front of the skull base.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These symptoms are non-specific and occur in many conditions — they should be discussed with a neurologist.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI with contrast</strong> — the main method. It shows the size and location of the tumour, brain swelling, and its relation to vessels and nerves. Meningioma typically enhances with contrast.</li>
      <li><strong>CT</strong> — better shows calcification in the tumour and bone changes; also used when MRI is contraindicated.</li>
      <li><strong>Angiography</strong> — before surgery, to assess the tumour's blood supply.</li>
      <li><strong>Histology</strong> of the removed tumour — the final diagnosis and WHO grade.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Meningioma grades</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grade 1</strong> — benign, 80–90% of cases. Slow-growing.</li>
      <li><strong>Grade 2</strong> — atypical. Grow more actively, recur more often, need closer follow-up.</li>
      <li><strong>Grade 3</strong> — malignant (anaplastic), rare. Radiotherapy is usually needed after surgery.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Observation</strong> — for a small tumour without symptoms, especially in older patients or with serious other illnesses. Follow-up — examination and MRI on schedule.</li>
      <li><strong>Surgery</strong> — the main method if the tumour grows, compresses the brain or causes symptoms.</li>
      <li><strong>Radiosurgery and radiotherapy</strong> — for small tumours in hard-to-reach places, after partial removal, for atypical and malignant forms.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>Surgery</h2>
    <p>The goal is to remove the tumour completely together with the patch of dura it is attached to: this gives the best prognosis. I operate microsurgically, under the microscope and with neuronavigation, which shows the position of instruments relative to the tumour in real time.</p>
    <p>The most complex meningiomas are at the skull base, where the tumour encases major arteries and the optic nerve. Such operations last many hours: the tumour is removed piece by piece, separating it from each vessel and nerve. If complete removal threatens vital structures, part of the tumour is left and then monitored or irradiated.</p>
